  urldate = {2026-06-28},
  abstract = {Enumerative invariants in Algebraic Geometry 'count' \${$\tau\$$}-(semi)stable objects \$E\$ with fixed topological invariants \$[E]=a\$ in some geometric problem, using a virtual class \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm virt\textbraceright\$ in homology, for the moduli spaces \$\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm st\textbraceright ({$\tau$})\textbackslash subseteq\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})\$ of \${$\tau\$$}-(semi)stable objects. We get numbers by taking integrals \$\textbackslash int\_\textbraceleft [\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm virt\textbraceright\textbraceright{$\Upsilon\$$} for cohomology classes \${$\Upsilon\$$}. Let \$\textbackslash cal A\$ be a \$\textbackslash mathbb C\$-linear abelian category in Algebraic Geometry. There are two moduli stacks of objects in \$\textbackslash cal A\$: the usual moduli stack \$\textbackslash cal M\$, and the 'projective linear' moduli stack \$\textbackslash cal M\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm pl\textbraceright\$. We give \$H\_*(\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright )\$ the structure of a vertex algebra, and \$H\_*(\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm pl\textbraceright )\$ a Lie algebra. Virtual classes \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm virt\textbraceright\$ lie in \$H\_*(\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm pl\textbraceright )\$. We develop a universal theory of enumerative invariants in such \$\textbackslash mathcal A\$. Virtual classes \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm virt\textbraceright\$ are only defined when \$\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm st\textbraceright ({$\tau$})=\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})\$. We define invariants \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm inv\textbraceright\$ in \$H\_*(\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm pl\textbraceright )\$ for all \$a\$, with \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm inv\textbraceright =[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm virt\textbraceright\$ when \$\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm st\textbraceright ({$\tau$})=\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})\$. If \${$\tau$},{$\tau$}'\$ are stability conditions on \$\textbackslash cal A\$, we prove a wall-crossing formula writing \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_a\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$}')]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm inv\textbraceright\$ in terms of the \$[\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\_b\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm ss\textbraceright ({$\tau$})]\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm inv\textbraceright\$, using the Lie bracket on \$H\_*(\textbraceleft\textbackslash cal M\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash rm pl\textbraceright )\$. We apply our results for \$\textbackslash cal A\$ the representations of a quiver or quiver with relations, or coh\$(X)\$ for \$X\$ a curve, surface or Fano 3-fold, or a category of 'pairs' in coh\$(X)\$ for \$X\$ a curve or surface. This proves conjectures in Gross-Joyce-Tanaka arXiv:2005.05637.},
